<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- ── THE LAUNCH SCREEN ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	     There was NO launch screen configured at all, so iOS painted a blank frame
	     between tap and first render — white in light mode, against a splash that is
	     near-black. That flash was the ugliest moment in the app.

	     UILaunchScreen (not a storyboard): the ground is LaunchBackground, pinned to
	     #0B0B0B in BOTH appearances so it matches SplashScreen's ground exactly and the
	     handoff is invisible.

	     The mark is LaunchMark — the APP ICON's V, lifted off its black square into
	     transparency so it composites onto the launch colour instead of showing a second
	     black rectangle with its own edges. It is NOT VoiidLogoMark: that asset is the
	     three-bar wordmark lockup, a different shape from the icon the user just tapped,
	     and seeing one become the other reads as the wrong app opening.

	     Keep this in step with SplashScreen. If its ground ever moves off #0B0B0B, this
	     colorset moves with it or the flash comes back. -->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>LaunchBackground</string>
		<key>UIImageName</key>
		<string>LaunchMark</string>
		<key>UIImageRespectsSafeAreaInsets</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<!-- Location. Feature (B) — the Map — is FOREGROUND-ONLY and needs only When-In-Use.
	     In-conversation live sharing (Feature A) has now landed and adds the Always string
	     below plus the `location` background mode (in UIBackgroundModes), so a user-started,
	     timer-bounded share keeps updating while Voiid is backgrounded (docs/LOCATION.md §5).
	     Apple rejects vague strings, so these are specific about WHAT is collected, WHEN, and
	     that it is end-to-end encrypted. Both strings must be present before Always can ever
	     be requested (iOS 11+). -->
	<!-- Taking a profile photo. Without this key iOS TERMINATES the app the instant the
	     camera is presented — not a permission denial, a crash. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Take a photo for your Voiid profile.</string>
	<!-- Voice notes, and the audio half of a call. Same rule as the camera above: WITHOUT
	     THIS KEY iOS TERMINATES THE APP the instant the microphone is requested. The
	     onboarding permissions screen asks for this in sequence, so a missing key does not
	     fail one row — it kills the app partway through the run. -->
	<key>NSMicrophoneUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Record voice notes and talk on Voiid calls. Audio is end-to-end encrypted — Voiid's servers never hear it.</string>
	<!-- Contact discovery. Same termination rule.
	     The string says what actually happens, because it is the most sensitive of the six:
	     matching is done against HASHES and the address book is never uploaded. -->
	<key>NSContactsUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Find friends already on Voiid. Your contacts are matched privately on your device and are never uploaded.</string>
	<!-- Reading from the library, to attach a photo or video to a chat. Same termination rule. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Choose photos and videos to send on Voiid. Everything you send is end-to-end encrypted.</string>
	<!-- Writing BACK to the library — saving a received photo, or a clip you recorded.
	     A separate key from the one above: iOS treats read and add as different grants, and
	     the add-only prompt is what appears when the app saves rather than picks. -->
	<key>NSPhotoLibraryAddUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Save photos and videos from Voiid to your library.</string>
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Voiid uses your location only when you choose to share it in a chat or on the Map. Your location is end-to-end encrypted — Voiid's servers never see it.</string>
	<key>NSLocationAlwaysAndWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>Allow Always so live location keeps updating while Voiid is in the background, for as long as you set. Sharing stops automatically when your timer ends.</string>
	<!-- Contact/dialer linking. Declaring INStartCallIntent is what lets iOS hand a
	     "call this person on Voiid" back to us: tapping a Voiid entry in Recents or
	     inside a contact card resumes the app with this activity type, and Siri can
	     act on "call <name> on Voiid". Without it the donated intents in CallManager
	     produce suggestions the system cannot deliver anywhere.
	     The two legacy types keep older call-back entries working. -->
	<key>NSUserActivityTypes</key>
	<array>
		<string>INStartCallIntent</string>
		<string>INStartAudioCallIntent</string>
		<string>INStartVideoCallIntent</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Background modes for calls. NOTE: these must live here, not as
	     INFOPLIST_KEY_UIBackgroundModes build settings — Xcode's generated-plist
	     mechanism does not recognise UIBackgroundModes and silently drops it.
	       voip                – PushKit wakes the app for an incoming call
	       audio               – keep call audio alive in the background
	       remote-notification – message pushes / NSE + Firebase Auth silent push
	       location            – keep a user-started, timer-bounded live location share
	                             updating while Voiid is backgrounded. allowsBackground-
	                             LocationUpdates TRAPS without this key; the share is never
	                             started on launch, only by an explicit user action with an
	                             explicit end time (docs/LOCATION.md §5). -->
	<key>UIBackgroundModes</key>
	<array>
		<string>voip</string>
		<string>audio</string>
		<string>remote-notification</string>
		<string>location</string>
	</array>
	<!-- Firebase Phone Auth's reCAPTCHA fallback returns through this custom URL scheme.
	     It is GOOGLE_APP_ID from GoogleService-Info.plist with ':' replaced by '-'.
	
	     IT MUST MATCH THE PLIST, and FirebaseAuth does not degrade gracefully when it does not:
	     PhoneAuthProvider.swift:112 calls fatalError, so tapping Continue killed the app outright
	     whenever silent APNs verification was unavailable and it fell back to reCAPTCHA.
	
	     The app briefly shipped GoogleService-Info.plist for the `voiid-prod` project while this
	     scheme named `voiid-native`. The APNs key is registered against voiid-native, so that is
	     the project the app uses and both values now name it. -->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleTypeRole</key>
			<string>Editor</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>app-1-104136277519-ios-d155569c969bffc86b6d7a</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
